---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/home/Hero.astro';
import SectionPanels, { type Panel } from '../../components/home/SectionPanels.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/home/SiteFooter.astro';
import { tools } from '../../lib/tools';
import { eventStormerUrl } from '../../lib/links';

/*
 * The tools. Definitions live in src/lib/tools.ts so the panel copy has exactly
 * one home.
 *
 * Two of them link *out*: the Context mapper and the landscape collector, whose
 * addresses are configuration read per request from the environment. That is
 * safe because this page is server-rendered; the prerendered /doc/* pages have
 * to go through /go/ instead.
 *
 * `href` is a function exactly when the address is configuration rather than a
 * route, which is why it is resolved here rather than in the data.
 *
 * One tool is deployed and the rest are not, so the notice near the top says
 * exactly that rather than a blanket "coming soon" — the panels carry their own
 * status badges, and the notice's job is to tell a reader which of the five to
 * click first.
 */
const panels: readonly Panel[] = tools.map((tool) => ({
	title: tool.title,
	description: tool.description,
	href: typeof tool.href === 'function' ? tool.href() : tool.href,
	cta: tool.cta,
	status: tool.status,
	icon: tool.icon,
	...(tool.external ? { external: true } : {}),
}));
---

<Layout
	title="Tools — BA Hub"
	description="The Context mapper and the tools around it: catalog in, context map drawn, landscape evidence ingested, and a conformance verdict against every edge."
>
	<main id="main">
		<Hero
			kicker="Tools"
			title="One tool for the wall the model is built on, and one for checking whether anyone still respects it."
			lead="The first exists. The Event Stormer runs event storming and process modelling on the
				same board — big picture for discovery, process modelling for the commands and
				policies, software design for the aggregates underneath — because they are three
				depths of one notation rather than three exercises. The second does not exist yet: a
				mapper that holds the catalog and the observed estate at the same time and computes
				the difference, so its interesting output is never the map but the list of edges the
				map got wrong. Everything here serves one of those two jobs."
			parent={{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }}
			actions={[
				{ label: 'Open the Event Stormer', href: eventStormerUrl(), external: true },
				{ label: 'Read the mapper design', href: '/doc/tooling/context-mapper/' },
			]}
		/>

		<div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-5xl px-6 pt-16">
			<!--
				Amber rather than the brand colour: this is a caveat, not a feature, and
				it should read as one on first glance. It is deliberately specific about
				*which* tool is real — a blanket "coming soon" over a box containing one
				working tool teaches the reader to ignore the badges.
			-->
			<p class="rounded-xl border border-amber-300 bg-amber-50 p-5 text-sm dark:border-amber-700/60 dark:bg-amber-950/40">
				<strong>One of these is deployed. The other four are not.</strong>
				<span class="mt-1 block text-ink-muted dark:text-slate-400">
					The <strong class="text-ink dark:text-slate-200">Event Stormer</strong> is real and
					usable now — it is doc-hub&rsquo;s board, and it runs two of this hub&rsquo;s
					practices rather than one: event storming and process modelling are two of its
					three levels. For the rest, the designs are written and the interfaces are decided;
					the software is not built. The addresses those panels point at are configuration,
					so the day something is deployed behind one it takes a value change and a restart
					rather than a rebuild. Until then, the
					<a href="/landscapes" class="font-semibold text-brand underline-offset-4 hover:underline dark:text-purple-400">
						landscapes
					</a>
					page does by hand what the mapper would do continuously.
				</span>
			</p>
		</div>

		<SectionPanels panels={panels} label="Tools" columns={3} />

		<section
			aria-labelledby="in-out"
			class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 py-16 sm:py-20 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-night-raised"
		>
			<div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-5xl px-6">
				<h2 id="in-out" class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What goes in, what comes out</h2>
				<p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-ink-muted dark:text-slate-400">
					A tool with no named input is a drawing program, and a tool with no named consumer is
					a hobby. Both columns are filled in for every entry below, which is a cheap way to
					notice when one of these should not be built.
				</p>

				<div class="mt-8 overflow-x-auto">
					<table class="w-full min-w-[48rem] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
						<thead>
							<tr class="border-b border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700">
								<th scope="col" class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold">Tool</th>
								<th scope="col" class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold">Does what nothing else does</th>
								<th scope="col" class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold">Reads</th>
								<th scope="col" class="py-3 font-semibold">Emits</th>
							</tr>
						</thead>
						<tbody>
							{
								tools.map((tool) => (
									<tr class="border-b border-slate-200 align-top dark:border-slate-800">
										<th scope="row" class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold whitespace-nowrap">
											{tool.title}
										</th>
										<td class="py-3 pr-4 text-ink-muted dark:text-slate-400">{tool.purpose}</td>
										<td class="py-3 pr-4 text-ink-muted dark:text-slate-400">{tool.input}</td>
										<td class="py-3 text-ink-muted dark:text-slate-400">{tool.output}</td>
									</tr>
								))
							}
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section aria-labelledby="mcp-note" class="mx-auto w-full max-w-5xl px-6 py-16 sm:py-20">
			<h2 id="mcp-note" class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">And the part a model can do</h2>
			<p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-ink-muted dark:text-slate-400">
				Some of this work is judgement and some of it is reading. Distilling a first pass at the
				subdomains out of documents the business already wrote, spotting that one word carries
				two meanings across four systems, or noticing that an integration nobody declared has
				appeared — none of those need an analyst, and all of them currently consume one.
			</p>
			<div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
				<a
					href="/doc/mcp/"
					class="rounded-full bg-brand px-6 py-3 font-semibold text-white transition hover:bg-brand-strong focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-3 focus-visible:outline-brand"
				>
					The four MCP servers
				</a>
				<a
					href="/doc/tooling/"
					class="rounded-full border border-slate-300 px-6 py-3 font-semibold transition hover:bg-white dark:border-slate-600 dark:hover:bg-night"
				>
					Tooling overview
				</a>
			</div>
		</section>
	</main>
	<SiteFooter />
</Layout>
